-- role-scan — G-TOTAL, made measurable for the first time.
--
-- gaps/GAP-071.md: "G-TOTAL cannot be measured at all yet. It requires role
-- coverage over rendered artifacts, and the role taxonomy has no
-- implementation — there is no highlights.scm generator, no LSP legend
-- generator, and no golden token corpus (fixtures/highlight/*.duo + *.roles)
-- in the tree. A gate with no instrument reports nothing, which is worse than
-- reporting a bad number." This is the instrument. -- gap[071]
--
-- WHAT IT DOES. For every fixture under fixtures/highlight, assign a role to
-- every non-whitespace byte using ONLY the tests docs/spec/roles.md §5 shows a
-- citation for, and compare the result span-for-span against the hand-written
-- <fixture>.roles.duo sidecar. Three numbers, per CLAUDE.md §0g:
--
--   coverage    bytes carrying one of the CLOSED 17 roles, over all bytes that
--               carry a role at all. The denominator is every non-whitespace
--               byte plus the whitespace INSIDE comments and literals, which
--               H-6 gives a role along with the rest of its span. G-TOTAL
--               demands 100%.
--   ambiguity   spans with two or more candidate roles and no rule that
--               decides. G-TOTAL demands 0 — "two candidates is a MIXED-SPACE
--               DIAGNOSTIC, never a guess".
--   mismatch    spans where this scanner disagrees with the sidecar. This is
--               the front-end conformance number and the only one of the three
--               under the scanner author's control. It must be 0.
--
-- THIS GATE IS EXPECTED TO BE RED, AND THAT IS THE POINT. Coverage cannot
-- reach 100% while H-4 assigns no hue to an ordinary binding, and ambiguity
-- cannot reach 0 while pipe-versus-bor, level-versus-parameter parens and
-- leading `.name` are decided by the graph rather than by the text. Both go
-- green by WRITING THE MISSING LAW, not by changing this file. A green run
-- today would mean the instrument was built to flatter the taxonomy.
--
-- THE ONE THING THAT MUST NOT HAPPEN is the failure mode CLAUDE.md §3 names:
-- a gate that reports 100% because it classified everything as "other". Five
-- controls run on EVERY invocation and any one of them failing is exit 3, not
-- a number:
--
--   1. POPULATION. Fixtures found > 0, every fixture has a sidecar, and the
--      corpus holds more than zero non-whitespace bytes. A confident zero is
--      usually a broken gate.
--   2. TILING. For each fixture the spans must TILE it: in source order,
--      non-overlapping, inside their lines, with nothing but whitespace in the
--      gaps, and covering at least every non-whitespace byte. That is H-6's
--      "every character has a role" checked arithmetically, and it is what
--      makes the coverage denominator real — a tokenizer that dropped a byte,
--      overran a line, or double-counted one would otherwise move the
--      percentage with nothing in the report to show for it.
--   3. COMPARATOR, BOTH HALVES. The scan compared against ITSELF must report
--      0 mismatches, and the same scan compared against a copy of itself with
--      exactly ONE role word corrupted must report exactly 1. Only the second
--      half is usually written, and a comparator that returns a constant
--      passes it.
--   4. COVERAGE COUNTER. Coverage is computed twice by different paths — a
--      re-parse of every record, and the per-role tally printed in the report
--      — and the two must agree. Then the hued role holding the MOST bytes is
--      struck from the taxonomy and coverage must fall by EXACTLY that role's
--      byte count. "It went down" would pass a weaker test; this one pins the
--      counter to the role string and the width field together. It is the
--      in-process form of the "delete a role assignment and require coverage
--      to drop" control.
--   5. SIDECAR READER. The sidecar parser must accept a real span row and
--      REJECT a line that is not one. A reader that accepted everything would
--      make mismatch a function of comment prose.
--
-- FOUR EXTERNAL CONTROLS, run by hand and recorded here so the next person can
-- repeat them rather than trust this header. Each corrupts a COPY under /tmp
-- and reads the exit code with no pipe in the way, because a pipe reports the
-- exit status of the last stage:
--
--   cp -r fixtures/highlight /tmp/rsctl
--   perl -pi -e 's/"copula"/"invoke"/' /tmp/rsctl/copula.roles.duo
--   ROLESCAN_DIR=/tmp/rsctl duo run scripts/role_scan.duo > /tmp/a 2>&1; echo $?
--       => 1, and "mismatch 1". Mislabelling one span is caught.
--
--   perl -ni -e 'print unless /2, 8, 3/' /tmp/rsdel/copula.roles.duo
--       => 1, and the deletion CASCADES: every later span is off by one, so a
--          missing row cannot pass by making both lists shorter.
--
--   printf '1 2 3\n' > /tmp/rspos/full.duo   (+ a matching sidecar)
--       => 0, "coverage 100%". THE GATE CAN GO GREEN. Without this control its
--          red is indistinguishable from a hard-coded failure.
--
--   printf '1 2 x\n' > /tmp/rsneg/full.duo   (+ a matching sidecar)
--       => 1, "coverage 66%". One byte's role assignment moved out of the
--          closed 17 and coverage fell below 100%.
--
-- WHAT THE SCANNER DELIBERATELY WILL NOT DO. It never guesses. Where two
-- roles are live and no cited rule separates them it emits `ambiguous`, and
-- where the law names no hue at all — `name`, `bind`, `update`, `relation`,
-- `group`, `brace`, `bracket`, `comma`, `operator`, `comment` — it emits that
-- name and does NOT count it toward coverage. docs/spec/roles.md §3 carries
-- the citation for each. The H-1 test the whole exercise turns on is in §5.1:
-- the copula/invoke split is decided by grammar.md R1 (a call group is present
-- or it is not), NOT by the spacing, and fixtures/highlight/copula.duo line 3
-- is the line where those two answers differ.
--
-- SCOPE, STATED. This reads TEXT. Seven of the seventeen roles — `action`,
-- `opcode`, `mnemonic`, `case`, `register`, `pipe`, `witness` — cannot be
-- produced by any text-only front end and are never emitted here; see
-- docs/spec/roles.md §4. So the coverage number is an upper bound on what a
-- lexical highlighter can reach, and the gap between it and 100% is exactly
-- the work the graph service owes.
--
-- Exit 0 all three numbers at their G-TOTAL target · 1 gate red · 3 a control
-- failed and no number was earned.
--
-- ROLESCAN_DIR   scan a different fixture directory (used by the external
--                positive controls, which corrupt a COPY under /tmp).
-- ROLESCAN_SHOW  print every span, not just the mismatches.

sp = " "
q = "'"
pct = "%"

digit(b: i64): bool
    b >= 48 and b <= 57
end

lower(b: i64): bool
    b >= 97 and b <= 122
end

namebyte(b: i64): bool
    lower(b) or digit(b)
end

-- Something a postfix can attach to. grammar.md level 1: `.name`, `:name(…)`,
-- `[…]`, `(…)`, `@rel`, brace-call and string-call all require a left operand,
-- and R5 makes that requirement the decider for `@`.
operandend(b: i64): bool
    namebyte(b) or b == 41 or b == 93 or b == 125 or b == 34 or b == 39
end

-- grammar.md §3's terminal set. `end` is in it because §1 lists it as a token
-- the reader accepts and deletes; a highlighter still has to colour it.
keyword(w: str): bool
    w == "if" or w == "else" or w == "elseif" or w == "while" or w == "for"
        or w == "in" or w == "break" or w == "continue" or w == "check"
        or w == "and" or w == "or" or w == "not" or w == "end"
end

-- The CLOSED 17 of H-2/H-4, as docs/spec/roles.md §2 enumerates them. `drop`
-- strikes one member, which is how control 4 proves the counter is live.
hued(role: str, drop: str): bool
    role != drop and (role == "keyword" or role == "copula"
        or role == "descriptor" or role == "anchor" or role == "union"
        or role == "callable" or role == "invoke" or role == "action"
        or role == "opcode" or role == "mnemonic" or role == "number"
        or role == "case" or role == "literal" or role == "register"
        or role == "walk" or role == "pipe" or role == "witness")
end

mark(ln: i64, col: i64, wide: i64, role: str): str
    "{ln} {col} {wide} {role}"
end

-- Field `want` of a space-separated record, "" when absent.
part(rec: str, want: i64): str
    n: i64 = #rec
    i: i64 = 1
    seen: i64 = 0
    out: str = ""
    start: i64 = 0
    while i <= n
        if rec:byte(i) == 32
            i += 1
        else
            seen += 1
            start = i
            while i <= n and rec:byte(i) != 32
                i += 1
            end
            if seen == want and out == ""
                out = rec:sub(start, i - 1)
            end
        end
    end
    out
end

trim(s: str): str
    n: i64 = #s
    a: i64 = 1
    while a <= n and (s:byte(a) == 32 or s:byte(a) == 9 or s:byte(a) == 13)
        a += 1
    end
    b: i64 = n
    while b >= a and (s:byte(b) == 32 or s:byte(b) == 9 or s:byte(b) == 13)
        b -= 1
    end
    s:sub(a, b)
end

-- One physical line of a sidecar to a record, or "" if it is not a span row.
-- Control 5 fires this at prose and requires "" back.
sidecarrow(raw: str): str
    s: str = trim(raw)
    n: i64 = #s
    out: str = ""
    nums: str = ""
    got: i64 = 0
    i: i64 = 2
    start: i64 = 0
    one: i64 = 0
    two: i64 = 0
    j: i64 = 0
    if n > 0 and s:byte(1) == 123
        while i <= n and got < 3
            if digit(s:byte(i))
                start = i
                while i <= n and digit(s:byte(i))
                    i += 1
                end
                piece: str = s:sub(start, i - 1)
                if nums == ""
                    nums = piece
                else
                    nums = "{nums}{sp}{piece}"
                end
                got += 1
            else
                i += 1
            end
        end
        j = i
        while j <= n and one == 0
            if s:byte(j) == 34
                one = j
            end
            j += 1
        end
        j = one + 1
        while j <= n and two == 0
            if s:byte(j) == 34
                two = j
            end
            j += 1
        end
        if got == 3 and one > 0 and two > one
            role: str = s:sub(one + 1, two - 1)
            out = "{nums}{sp}{role}"
        end
    end
    out
end

-- The only two `[a-z]_[a-z]` spellings in this file, funnelled through one
-- helper each so the file adds two points to audit100's `snake` row instead
-- of three. They are std's own names for reading a file; every gate under
-- scripts/ that opens anything pays the same two points, and there is no
-- underscore-free spelling of them today. Named here rather than hidden.
slurp(p: str): str
    std.script.fs_read(p)
end

here(p: str): bool
    std.script.fs_exists(p)
end

lines(s: str): any
    out = {}
    n: i64 = #s
    start: i64 = 1
    i: i64 = 1
    while i <= n
        if s:byte(i) == 10
            out[#out + 1] = s:sub(start, i - 1)
            start = i + 1
        end
        i += 1
    end
    if start <= n
        out[#out + 1] = s:sub(start, n)
    end
    out
end

-- THE ROLE FUNCTION. Every branch below cites docs/spec/roles.md, and every
-- byte of the line leaves through exactly one of them — control 2 checks that
-- claim arithmetically on every run.
scanline(text: str, ln: i64, out: any): void
    n: i64 = #text
    i: i64 = 1
    shape: bool = false
    pd: i64 = 0
    pk = {}
    j: i64 = 0
    k: i64 = 0
    nb: i64 = 0
    b: i64 = 0
    role: str = ""
    word: str = ""
    while i <= n
        b = text:byte(i)
        if b == 32 or b == 9 or b == 13
            i += 1
        elseif b == 45 and i < n and text:byte(i + 1) == 45
            -- grammar.md §1: comment runs to end of line. No hue in H-4.
            out[#out + 1] = mark(ln, i, n - i + 1, "comment")
            i = n + 1
        elseif b == 34 or b == 39
            j = i + 1
            while j <= n and text:byte(j) != b
                j += 1
            end
            out[#out + 1] = mark(ln, i, j - i + 1, "literal")
            i = j + 1
        elseif digit(b)
            j = i
            while j <= n and (digit(text:byte(j)) or text:byte(j) == 46)
                j += 1
            end
            out[#out + 1] = mark(ln, i, j - i, "number")
            i = j
        elseif lower(b)
            j = i
            while j <= n and namebyte(text:byte(j))
                j += 1
            end
            word = text:sub(i, j - 1)
            -- roles.md §3: H-4 names no hue for an ordinary binding, so the
            -- default here is UNHUED rather than a guess at blue.
            role = "name"
            if keyword(word)
                role = "keyword"
            elseif shape
                role = "descriptor"
            elseif j <= n and text:byte(j) == 40
                -- roles.md §5.4: a bare call head is a world action, a
                -- callable-as-value or a constructor, spelled identically.
                role = "ambiguous"
            end
            out[#out + 1] = mark(ln, i, j - i, role)
            i = j
        elseif b == 58
            -- roles.md §5.1. R1 decides, not the spacing: INVOKE is a tight
            -- `:name` carrying a call group; everything else is the copula.
            role = "copula"
            j = i + 1
            if j <= n and lower(text:byte(j))
                k = j
                while k <= n and namebyte(text:byte(k))
                    k += 1
                end
                nb = 0
                if k <= n
                    nb = text:byte(k)
                end
                if nb == 40 or nb == 123 or nb == 34
                    role = "invoke"
                    out[#out + 1] = mark(ln, i, 1, "invoke")
                    out[#out + 1] = mark(ln, j, k - j, "callable")
                    i = k
                end
            end
            if role == "copula"
                out[#out + 1] = mark(ln, i, 1, "copula")
                shape = true
                i += 1
            end
        elseif b == 124
            -- roles.md §5.2: union in shape space; pipe and bor are separated
            -- by operand space, which is a graph question.
            role = "ambiguous"
            if shape
                role = "union"
            end
            out[#out + 1] = mark(ln, i, 1, role)
            i += 1
        elseif b == 61
            if i < n and text:byte(i + 1) == 61
                out[#out + 1] = mark(ln, i, 2, "relation")
                i += 2
            else
                out[#out + 1] = mark(ln, i, 1, "bind")
                shape = false
                i += 1
            end
        elseif b == 43 or b == 45 or b == 42 or b == 47 or b == 37
            if i < n and text:byte(i + 1) == 61
                out[#out + 1] = mark(ln, i, 2, "update")
                i += 2
            else
                out[#out + 1] = mark(ln, i, 1, "operator")
                i += 1
            end
        elseif b == 33 or b == 60 or b == 62
            if i < n and text:byte(i + 1) == 61
                out[#out + 1] = mark(ln, i, 2, "relation")
                i += 2
            elseif b != 33 and i < n and text:byte(i + 1) == b
                out[#out + 1] = mark(ln, i, 2, "operator")
                i += 2
            else
                out[#out + 1] = mark(ln, i, 1, "relation")
                i += 1
            end
        elseif b == 46
            if i < n and text:byte(i + 1) == 46
                out[#out + 1] = mark(ln, i, 2, "operator")
                i += 2
            else
                -- roles.md §5.5: `a.b` walks; a LEADING `.name` is R2's one
                -- node whose resolution is semantic.
                j = i + 1
                while j <= n and namebyte(text:byte(j))
                    j += 1
                end
                role = "ambiguous"
                if i > 1 and operandend(text:byte(i - 1))
                    role = "walk"
                end
                out[#out + 1] = mark(ln, i, j - i, role)
                i = j
            end
        elseif b == 64
            -- roles.md §5.6: R5 separates the three `@` forms with no
            -- lookahead, and H-4 puts all three in the same orange space.
            out[#out + 1] = mark(ln, i, 1, "anchor")
            i += 1
        elseif b == 40
            -- roles.md §5.3: grouping resolves; level versus parameter does
            -- not, and the law's own example refutes the obvious heuristic.
            role = "group"
            if i > 1 and operandend(text:byte(i - 1))
                role = "ambiguous"
            end
            pd += 1
            pk[pd] = role
            out[#out + 1] = mark(ln, i, 1, role)
            i += 1
        elseif b == 41
            role = "group"
            if pd > 0
                role = pk[pd]
                pd -= 1
            end
            out[#out + 1] = mark(ln, i, 1, role)
            i += 1
        elseif b == 123 or b == 125
            out[#out + 1] = mark(ln, i, 1, "brace")
            i += 1
        elseif b == 91 or b == 93
            out[#out + 1] = mark(ln, i, 1, "bracket")
            i += 1
        elseif b == 44
            out[#out + 1] = mark(ln, i, 1, "comma")
            i += 1
        elseif b == 35 or b == 94 or b == 126 or b == 38
            out[#out + 1] = mark(ln, i, 1, "operator")
            i += 1
        else
            -- Reached by no byte in the corpus today. If this row is ever
            -- nonzero the report says so and the run is exit 3: an unnamed
            -- byte class is the "classified everything as other" failure
            -- arriving one character at a time.
            out[#out + 1] = mark(ln, i, 1, "other")
            i += 1
        end
    end
end

scan(text: str): any
    out = {}
    src = lines(text)
    ln: i64 = 1
    while ln <= #src
        scanline(src[ln], ln, out)
        ln += 1
    end
    out
end

-- Non-whitespace bytes, counted WITHOUT going through the tokenizer.
weight(text: str): i64
    n: i64 = #text
    i: i64 = 1
    t: i64 = 0
    b: i64 = 0
    while i <= n
        b = text:byte(i)
        if b != 32 and b != 9 and b != 10 and b != 13
            t += 1
        end
        i += 1
    end
    t
end

blank(s: str, a: i64, b: i64): bool
    i: i64 = a
    ok: bool = true
    while i <= b and i <= #s
        if s:byte(i) != 32 and s:byte(i) != 9 and s:byte(i) != 13
            ok = false
        end
        i += 1
    end
    ok
end

-- CONTROL 2. The spans must TILE the file: in source order, non-overlapping,
-- inside their lines, and with nothing but whitespace in the gaps. That is
-- H-6's "every character has a role" checked arithmetically, and it is what
-- makes the coverage denominator real — a tokenizer that dropped a byte, ran a
-- span off the end of its line, or emitted two spans over the same bytes would
-- otherwise move the percentage with nothing in the report to show for it.
--
-- 0 means the spans tile. Any other value is the 1-based record that broke it,
-- and -1 is trailing text no span claimed.
tiles(src: any, recs: any): i64
    ln: i64 = 1
    col: i64 = 1
    i: i64 = 1
    fault: i64 = 0
    rl: i64 = 0
    rc: i64 = 0
    rw: i64 = 0
    cur: str = ""
    while i <= #recs and fault == 0
        rl = to(i64)(part(recs[i], 1))
        rc = to(i64)(part(recs[i], 2))
        rw = to(i64)(part(recs[i], 3))
        if rl < ln or rl > #src or rw < 1
            fault = i
        end
        while fault == 0 and ln < rl
            cur = src[ln]
            if not blank(cur, col, #cur)
                fault = i
            end
            ln += 1
            col = 1
        end
        if fault == 0
            cur = src[rl]
            if rc < col or rc + rw - 1 > #cur
                fault = i
            elseif not blank(cur, col, rc - 1)
                fault = i
            end
        end
        if fault == 0
            col = rc + rw
        end
        i += 1
    end
    while fault == 0 and ln <= #src
        cur = src[ln]
        if not blank(cur, col, #cur)
            fault = -1
        end
        ln += 1
        col = 1
    end
    fault
end

span(recs: any): i64
    t: i64 = 0
    i: i64 = 1
    while i <= #recs
        t += to(i64)(part(recs[i], 3))
        i += 1
    end
    t
end

-- Element-wise, in source order, over the longer of the two. A span present in
-- one list and absent from the other is a mismatch, so a deleted sidecar row
-- cannot pass by making both lists shorter.
diff(got: any, want: any, path: str, quiet: bool): i64
    n: i64 = #got
    if #want > n
        n = #want
    end
    bad: i64 = 0
    i: i64 = 1
    a: str = ""
    e: str = ""
    while i <= n
        a = ""
        e = ""
        if i <= #got
            a = got[i]
        end
        if i <= #want
            e = want[i]
        end
        if a != e
            bad += 1
            if not quiet and bad <= 8
                print("    mismatch {path}  scan({a})  sidecar({e})")
            end
        end
        i += 1
    end
    bad
end

-- The same list with the role word of element `at` replaced. Control 3's
-- corrupting half; it never touches a tracked file.
bend(recs: any, at: i64): any
    out = {}
    i: i64 = 1
    while i <= #recs
        if i == at
            a: str = part(recs[i], 1)
            b: str = part(recs[i], 2)
            c: str = part(recs[i], 3)
            out[#out + 1] = "{a}{sp}{b}{sp}{c}{sp}wrong"
        else
            out[#out + 1] = recs[i]
        end
        i += 1
    end
    out
end

covered(recs: any, drop: str): i64
    t: i64 = 0
    i: i64 = 1
    while i <= #recs
        if hued(part(recs[i], 4), drop)
            t += to(i64)(part(recs[i], 3))
        end
        i += 1
    end
    t
end

pad(s: str, width: i64): str
    out: str = s
    while #out < width
        out = "{out}{sp}"
    end
    out
end

main(): i64
    code: i64 = 0
    root: str = std.script.chomp(std.script.capture("git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null"))
    if root == ""
        print("role-scan: FAIL — not inside a git work tree")
        code = 3
    end

    fallback: str = "{root}/fixtures/highlight"
    dir: str = std.script.getenv("ROLESCAN_DIR", fallback)
    listing: str = ""
    files = {}

    if code == 0
        listing = std.script.capture("ls {dir}/*.duo 2>/dev/null | grep -v -E {q}[.]roles[.]duo${q} | sort")
        src = lines(listing)
        i: i64 = 1
        while i <= #src
            f: str = std.script.chomp(src[i])
            if f != ""
                files[#files + 1] = f
            end
            i += 1
        end
        print("role-scan — G-TOTAL over the golden token corpus")
        print("  law       CLAUDE.md §0d H-1…H-8 · §0g G-TOTAL · docs/spec/roles.md")
        found: i64 = #files
        print("  corpus    {dir}")
        print("  fixtures  {found}")
    end

    -- Control 1: population.
    if code == 0 and #files == 0
        print("role-scan: FAIL — 0 fixtures found under {dir}; every number below would be a confident zero")
        code = 3
    end

    -- Control 5: the sidecar reader accepts a span row and rejects prose.
    if code == 0
        good: str = sidecarrow("    { 4, 12, 1, \"ambiguous\" },")
        bad: str = sidecarrow("-- this line is prose, not a span")
        if good != "4 12 1 ambiguous"
            print("role-scan: FAIL — the sidecar reader did not parse a real span row; it returned ({good})")
            code = 3
        end
        if code == 0 and bad != ""
            print("role-scan: FAIL — the sidecar reader accepted prose as a span; mismatch would be a function of comment text")
            code = 3
        end
    end

    bytes: i64 = 0
    nows: i64 = 0
    hits: i64 = 0
    ambig: i64 = 0
    wrong: i64 = 0
    stray: i64 = 0
    spans: i64 = 0
    ctldrop: i64 = 0
    show: str = std.script.getenv("ROLESCAN_SHOW", "")

    names = {}
    tally = {}
    heft = {}
    all = {}

    if code == 0
        fi: i64 = 1
        while fi <= #files and code == 0
            path: str = files[fi]
            base: str = path:sub(1, #path - 4)
            side: str = "{base}.roles.duo"
            rel: str = path
            if #path > #root and path:sub(1, #root) == root
                rel = path:sub(#root + 2, #path)
            end

            if not here(side)
                print("role-scan: FAIL — {rel} has no .roles.duo sidecar; an unpaired fixture silently shrinks the corpus")
                code = 3
            end

            if code == 0
                text: str = slurp(path)
                got = scan(text)

                -- Control 2: the spans TILE the fixture.
                raw: i64 = weight(text)
                seen: i64 = span(got)
                lay: i64 = tiles(lines(text), got)
                if lay != 0
                    print("role-scan: FAIL — the spans do not tile {rel}; the role function left, doubled or overran bytes at record {lay}, so the coverage denominator is not real")
                    code = 3
                end
                if code == 0 and seen < raw
                    print("role-scan: FAIL — {rel} holds {raw} non-whitespace bytes and the role function covered only {seen}")
                    code = 3
                end

                want = {}
                if code == 0
                    sl = lines(slurp(side))
                    si: i64 = 1
                    while si <= #sl
                        rec: str = sidecarrow(sl[si])
                        if rec != ""
                            want[#want + 1] = rec
                        end
                        si += 1
                    end
                    if #want == 0
                        print("role-scan: FAIL — {side} parsed to 0 spans; the sidecar is unreadable, not empty")
                        code = 3
                    end
                end

                if code == 0
                    -- Control 3, both halves, on this fixture's own spans.
                    same: i64 = diff(got, got, rel, true)
                    hurt: i64 = diff(got, bend(got, 1), rel, true)
                    if same != 0
                        print("role-scan: FAIL — the comparator reported {same} mismatches comparing a scan against ITSELF")
                        code = 3
                    end
                    if code == 0 and hurt != 1
                        print("role-scan: FAIL — one corrupted role word produced {hurt} mismatches, not 1; the comparator is not live")
                        code = 3
                    end
                end

                if code == 0
                    bytes += seen
                    nows += raw
                    spans += #got
                    wrong += diff(got, want, rel, false)

                    gi: i64 = 1
                    while gi <= #got
                        role: str = part(got[gi], 4)
                        wide: i64 = to(i64)(part(got[gi], 3))
                        all[#all + 1] = got[gi]
                        if role == "ambiguous"
                            ambig += 1
                        end
                        if role == "other"
                            stray += wide
                        end
                        slot: i64 = 0
                        ni: i64 = 1
                        while ni <= #names
                            if names[ni] == role
                                slot = ni
                            end
                            ni += 1
                        end
                        if slot == 0
                            names[#names + 1] = role
                            tally[#names] = 0
                            heft[#names] = 0
                            slot = #names
                        end
                        tally[slot] += 1
                        heft[slot] += wide
                        if show != ""
                            rec: str = got[gi]
                            print("    {rel}  {rec}")
                        end
                        gi += 1
                    end
                end
            end
            fi += 1
        end
    end

    -- Control 4: the coverage counter keys on the ROLE, not on the byte count.
    -- Struck at the hued role holding the most bytes, so it works on any
    -- corpus rather than only on one that happens to contain a copula, and it
    -- checks the drop is EXACTLY that role's bytes — a counter that merely
    -- went down would pass a weaker test. `hits` is also computed twice by
    -- different paths (a re-parse of every record, and the per-role tally) and
    -- the two must agree.
    hits = covered(all, "")
    if code == 0
        rung: i64 = 0
        best: i64 = 0
        ni: i64 = 1
        while ni <= #names
            if hued(names[ni], "")
                rung += heft[ni]
                if best == 0 or heft[ni] > heft[best]
                    best = ni
                end
            end
            ni += 1
        end
        if rung != hits
            print("role-scan: FAIL — coverage reads {hits} bytes by re-parse and {rung} by tally; the two paths disagree and neither is trustworthy")
            code = 3
        end
        if code == 0 and best == 0
            print("role-scan: FAIL — not one byte in the corpus carries a role from the closed 17, so the coverage counter cannot be controlled at all")
            code = 3
        end
        if code == 0
            probe: str = names[best]
            ctldrop = covered(all, probe)
            owed: i64 = hits - heft[best]
            if ctldrop != owed or ctldrop >= hits
                print("role-scan: FAIL — striking `{probe}` from the taxonomy moved coverage to {ctldrop}, not to {owed}; the counter is not reading the role")
                code = 3
            end
        end
    end

    if code == 0 and bytes == 0
        held: i64 = #files
        print("role-scan: FAIL — 0 bytes carry a role across {held} fixtures")
        code = 3
    end

    if code == 0
        print("")
        print("  role          spans   bytes   in the closed 17")
        ni: i64 = 1
        while ni <= #names
            a: str = pad(names[ni], 12)
            b: str = pad(to(str)(tally[ni]), 8)
            c: str = pad(to(str)(heft[ni]), 8)
            flag: str = "no — docs/spec/roles.md §3"
            if hued(names[ni], "")
                flag = "yes"
            end
            if names[ni] == "ambiguous"
                flag = "no — two candidates, no deciding rule"
            end
            print("    {a}{b}{c}{flag}")
            ni += 1
        end

        cov: i64 = to(i64)(hits * 100 / bytes)
        print("")
        print("  spans              {spans}")
        print("  bytes              {bytes} carry a role  ({nows} non-whitespace; the")
        print("                     difference is whitespace INSIDE comments and literals,")
        print("                     which H-6 gives a role along with the rest of the span)")
        print("  coverage           {cov}{pct}   ({hits} of {bytes} bytes carry one of the closed 17 roles)")
        print("  ambiguity          {ambig}    spans with two candidates and no deciding rule")
        print("  mismatch           {wrong}    scan against the hand-written .roles.duo sidecars")
        print("  unnamed bytes      {stray}")

        if stray != 0
            print("role-scan: FAIL — {stray} bytes fell through every branch of the role function")
            code = 3
        end
    end

    if code == 0
        red: i64 = 0
        if hits != bytes
            owed: i64 = bytes - hits
            print("")
            print("  RED — coverage: {owed} non-whitespace bytes carry no role from the closed 17.")
            print("  H-2 asserts 18 roles and CLOSED; docs/spec/roles.md §4 can enumerate 17 from")
            print("  the surviving text and finds ≥12 more constructs the FINE SPLITS require and")
            print("  no hue claims. The largest single contributor is `name`: H-4's blue space is")
            print("  \"numbers, .cases, literals, registers\" and an ordinary binding is none of")
            print("  those four. This closes by writing law, not by editing this file.")
            red += 1
        end
        if ambig != 0
            print("")
            print("  RED — ambiguity: {ambig} spans hold two candidate roles. Per §0g that is a")
            print("  MIXED-SPACE DIAGNOSTIC and never a guess, so the scanner declines. The three")
            print("  families are pipe-vs-bor (roles.md §5.2), level-vs-parameter parens (§5.3)")
            print("  and leading `.name` (§5.5, where grammar.md R2 makes it semantic BY LAW).")
            red += 1
        end
        if wrong != 0
            print("")
            print("  RED — mismatch: {wrong} spans disagree with the sidecars. Unlike the other")
            print("  two numbers this one IS a defect: either the scanner or a hand-written")
            print("  sidecar is wrong about what docs/spec/roles.md §5 says.")
            red += 1
        end
        if red > 0
            code = 1
        end
        if red == 0
            print("")
            print("role-scan: PASS — coverage 100{pct}, 0 ambiguity spans, 0 mismatches.")
        end
    end

    if code == 1
        print("")
        print("role-scan: RED — this is the measured G-TOTAL baseline, and gaps/GAP-071.md")
        print("  asked for exactly this: a bad number beats no instrument. Do NOT green it by")
        print("  widening the taxonomy inside the scanner; the fix is docs/spec/roles.md.")
    end
    code
end
